2. Standard precautions are applied to every patient, at all times

Staff at {{HOSPITAL_NAME}} do not decide whether to apply standard precautions on the basis of a patient's diagnosis, appearance, test result or presumed risk. Standard precautions are applied to every patient during every episode of care, on the working assumption that blood, all body fluids, secretions and excretions (excluding sweat), non-intact skin and mucous membranes may carry transmissible agents whether or not any infection has been identified.

The elements of standard precautions practised at {{HOSPITAL_NAME}} are:
- hand hygiene;
- personal protective equipment selected according to the anticipated exposure (gloves, gowns/aprons, masks, eye protection or face shield);
- respiratory hygiene and cough etiquette;
- safe injection practice;
- sharps safety, achieved through both device selection and work practice;
- safe handling and decontamination of used patient-care equipment and instruments;
- cleaning and disinfection of the care environment and frequently touched surfaces, including prompt management of blood and body fluid spills;
- safe handling of used linen; and
- segregation and disposal of biomedical waste.

A laminated summary of these elements is displayed at every nursing station and in every procedure room of {{HOSPITAL_NAME}}.

27. Antimicrobial usage policy — content, approval and availability

{{HOSPITAL_NAME}} maintains a written antimicrobial usage policy, approved by the ICC together with the pharmacy and the medical committee, and issued with a version number, an effective date and a review date.

The policy sets out:
- recommended empirical regimens for the common infection syndromes treated at {{HOSPITAL_NAME}}, chosen on the basis of the hospital's own antibiogram rather than on published national averages alone;
- the list of restricted antimicrobial agents that require prior authorisation before dispensing, and who may authorise them;
- criteria and timing for de-escalation from broad-spectrum to narrow-spectrum therapy once culture and sensitivity results are available;
- criteria for switching from intravenous to oral therapy;
- expected duration of therapy for common indications, and a rule that an end date or review date is recorded with every antimicrobial order;
- surgical antimicrobial prophylaxis — agent, dose, timing before incision, intra-operative re-dosing, and the maximum duration after surgery;
- dose adjustment in renal and hepatic impairment; and
- the requirement to send appropriate cultures before starting antimicrobial therapy wherever the clinical situation allows.

{{HOSPITAL_NAME}} may adopt the WHO AWaRe classification (Access, Watch, Reserve) to structure this list and to set targets for the proportion of use drawn from the Access group. A current copy of the policy is available in every clinical area as a printed quick-reference or on the hospital system, and every prescriber acknowledges it in writing at induction. The list of restricted agents is displayed in each ward and in the pharmacy.

32. Records maintained under this policy

{{HOSPITAL_NAME}} maintains, and retains for the period defined in its records retention policy — [Hospital to define]:
- hand hygiene compliance observation sheets and calculated compliance rates by area and staff category;
- handrub and soap dispenser check and refill logs;
- standard precautions spot-check and audit records for each clinical area;
- the transmission-based precaution assignment register, including start and discontinuation dates and authorising person;
- terminal cleaning records for isolation rooms;
- intravascular device insertion and maintenance bundle audit checklists;
- staff immunisation records, including hepatitis B vaccination dates, anti-HBs results, revaccination where applicable, and signed declination forms;
- the sharps and needlestick injury register, with source testing, investigation and post-exposure prophylaxis records;
- blood and body fluid spill incident records and spill kit replenishment logs;
- healthcare-associated infection surveillance data and the rates derived from it;
- biomedical waste segregation audit records, daily waste quantity records, and handover documents to the treatment facility;
- the restricted antimicrobial approval register;
- antimicrobial consumption reports and prescription audit registers;
- the cumulative antibiogram and evidence of its review;
- AST charter, meeting records, prospective audit and feedback recommendations, and outcome metric trends;
- ICC meeting minutes recording findings, named corrective actions, owners, due dates and verification of closure; and
- training attendance registers and competency assessment records.
